From 59ec53498bba719cda2b211b0594fd1639273c8b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Reminiscent <y870414423@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 16:42:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH NOT FOR COMMIT] Add exact-cardinality support for join-order
 benchmarks

This patch provides benchmark-only support for controlled join-order experiments.

It is not proposed for inclusion in PostgreSQL.

Apply it after the community join-order patch series being evaluated.

The benchmark disables Merge Join because exactcard does not model rows
before post-merge filters.

Formula mode also uses exact path-cost dominance instead of PostgreSQL's
normal 1% fuzz.  With the benchmark's large intermediate cardinalities, that
fuzz can discard paths whose cost differences are precisely the signal being
compared and can let a heuristic plan below the resulting DP anchor.  This
change is confined to formula mode; off mode retains the standard behavior.

The expected application base is the evaluated join-order series head
8293bde3fe3bdb6a6e2522413432d7209e2aa84d (Add a GUC
goo_greedy_strategy to choose GOO greedy strategies).  This is not maintained
as a compatibility patch for other revisions or patch series; resolve conflicts
manually when applying it elsewhere.

The main data flow is:

  generated relation/edge metadata
    -> inject base rows and pages
    -> derive every joinrel row count from its complete relation subset
    -> give Hash Join the corresponding output tuple count
    -> expose structured audit evidence to the benchmark

For review, start with the file header of exactcard.c, then inspect
exactcard_set_baserel_size(), exactcard_set_joinrel_rows(), and
exactcard_get_hashjoin_rows().  exactcard_last_audit() is the qualification
interface.  The regression SQL covers the externally visible contract; the
remaining catalog, GUC, and build-file changes are integration wiring.
